{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511334-ashirra","id":1511334,"title":"Ashirra?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-11T00:14:47-07:00","id":1511334,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Ashirra?","content":"Is there anyone working on anything for the Ashirra for V5? I'm currently working on writing some lore for Tehran for a play by post setting. I just wanted to reach out to anyone who is or was working on Ashirra material, be it for something large or small.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12573,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12573-kueijin","user":"Kueijin","date":". 05-11-2023, 11:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12573-kueijin","name":"Kueijin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12573_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-11T02:18:37-07:00","id":1511337,"title":"","content":"The following is from a Cairo game I was ST'ing . The metaplot is quite different from the official one, by you may still find useful ideas<br /> <br /> THE ASHIRRA<br /> The Ashirra is a vampire sect that gathers all vampires who declare their faith in Allah and believe that redemption for the Kindred was one of the promises He made to the Prophet Muhammad, Peace Be Upon Him.<br /> This sect was founded in the first years of Islam and, as the religion it was based on, spread quickly from Spain and North Africa to India and beyond. Following the Islamic model of political organization, and very much unlike the Camarilla, the Ashirra has always abhorred strongly centralized institutions, and relies on a system of local Emirs or Sultans exerting direct power and assisted in spiritual matters by Ulemas and Mullahs, who form a loose confederation from all Ashirra lands. Only in counted occasions a powerful religious leader will claim the title of Supreme Imam, and his authority would only extend to those who respected him on his own merits and personal virtue. No one has claimed the title, at least seriously, since the 16th century.<br /> The Ashirra has been the majority sect in the core lands of Islam since, but it lost a lot of its influence during the era of great European expansion, when the Camarilla tenets of secular humanism and centralized power expanded all over the globe and were adopted by many younger Muslim Kindred. By the end of the 20th century Ashirra were virtually indistinguishable from Camarilla Kindred, except by the fact that barely any Kindred identifying as a Muslim could be found among the Camarilla Justicar, Archon or any other sect-wide positions.<br /> The Ashirra as a sect took back the centre stage in the Muslim Kindred society during the events of the Year of the Herald, when powerful Elders from the previous ages used their faith as a rallying point to fight against the Sabbat, Gehena cults, demon worshipers and other terrors of the night. On the mortal side, the reaction to the war on terror policy gave young Muslims a renewed sense of identity, which they brought into the night when they got Embraced. As a result, nowdays the sect is, once again, the main player in the Muslim world. The Ashirra follow the exact same Six Traditions as the Camarilla, even if sometimes interpreted or implemented in a different way. For example, the role of the Elder outlined in the Traditions is often not fulfilled by the Prince, but by the Clan Elder, as it used to be during the Middle Ages. <br /> On top of the Traditions, the Ashirra also follow the Five Pillars of Islam. Obviously, because of their condition as vampires, some of these pillars have to be adapted or compromised on by the Ashirra. Fasting is followed in a lot of different ways, and most Ashirra only pray three times a night, at the times that are suitable for then. The Pilgrimage is a difficult endeavour because of the strong Faith of Mecca and most Ashirra prepare for years before undertaking it. But no Kindred would call themself Ashirra without at least the Profession of Faith.\u200b<br /> Most Ashirra follow Tariq el-Umma (the Road of the Community), a philosophical path similar enough to Humanity to be fully accepted by the Camarilla without any problems. Its base tenets are practically the same, and the higher ones merely emphasize the beleiver's duty to the community more than to an abstract sense of self. Others, specially among the Hajj, follow Sharia el-Sama' (the Law of Heaven), a path that emphasizes the believer's duty to Allah and can sometimes justify &quot;righteous violence&quot;. More worrying are the rumours of groups of Ashirra secretly following Sharia el-Harb (the Law of War), a modern adaptation of the tenets of jyhad against the infidels; this path is considered an aberration by most mainstream Ashirra and its adepts are shunned, ostracized or actively persecuted by most sect members.<br /> Because of its decentralized structure, the Ashirra is theoretically open to every Clan and Bloodline and doesn't have a clear divide between Founding Clans and others. On the other hand, Lineage is as important as personal virtue, and having Ancestors who are well known for their devotion to the sect can help open some doors and similarly, coming from a &quot;tainted&quot; Lineage can bar many more; in this particular, Clan Tremere and Giovanni are seen as aberrations by the Ashirra Elders who remember the Middle Ages and their usurpations, and a member of these Clans would only be allowed to join the Umma if they fully renounced their Lineage and became a Caitiff. The Clanless are not openly discriminated against, by their lack of Lineage usually works against them. <br /> As much as the Ashirra would like to present themselves as a spiritual community, their are not beyond engaging in power plays, political manouvering or even open internal conflict. As their Camarilla counterparts, they try to influence or control mortal institutions and often use them in proxy conflicts against each other.<br /> There are different factions among the Ashirra, based mostly on their specific beliefs.<br /> The Progressives are mostly secular, culturally Muslim Kindred who don't follow the Pillars very strictly. Most of them are very much like Camarilla Kindred, and sometimes have either been Embraced by them or Sired one of them.<br /> The Moderates don't necessarily have the middle ground in every issue. Instead, they treat every point of debate isolatedly and try to arrive to the right conclusion, following the style of al-Azhar Mosque and University. <br /> The Conservatives look at Tradition as the source of law. They usually follow wahhabism or other conservative Muslim school and apply it's principles to Kindred unlife.<br /> The Hajj are an ultraconservative faction formed mostly by Bay't Mutasharid, who see themselves as guardians of the holy places and monsters for the infidels. Unlike regular conservatives, they have their own school of thought, that, among others extreme principles, forbids the use of Obfuscate. <br /> Banu Haqim usually follow their own branch of Islam, based in Ismailist Shi'a, which encourages mysticism, devotion and the principle of Taqiya (Simulation of Conformity).<br /> The Shepherds of Islam are a diminute but influential group of Elders dedicated to the preservation of the Traditions of Islam. They are fairly conservative but try to avoid conflict with any with other Ashirra and even monotheistic or agnostic Kindred. On the other hand, they firmly oppose paganism and other pre abrahamic religions.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-11T05:20:05-07:00","id":1511346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kueijin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511334-ashirra#post1511334\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is there anyone working on anything for the Ashirra?.</div> </div> </div> </div>You mean for V5 or just in general, modern days? <br /> <b>Veil of Night</b>, <b>Cairo by Night</b> and in a sense<b> State of Grace</b> have the details, like history, Political Terms and status, Roads and Paths, Clans, Factions, and Bloodlines covered<br /> <br /> To be fair, It is never mentioned what led to the decline of the Ashirra. The sect spread into Asia with Muslim traders, establishing themselves on the Indian subcontinent and the Malay archipelago as far east as Mindanao despite opposition from both local Hindu Cainites and the Wan Kuei. They even managed to establish Sultans in Mumbai, Delhi, and Lahore. As late as the 19th century, the Ashirra were still vying for influence in Egypt, the Holy Land, and the steadily declining Ottoman Empire. By the modern era, their influence is greatly reduced and the Ashirra have entered a truce with the Camarilla to preserve what power they have left. The sect is strongest in Saudi Arabia, Iran, and Afghanistan, though it has some influence in Egypt as well.<br /> <br /> In V5 the Ashirra and the Camarilla have formed an alliance, symbolically represented by the Vermilion Wedding. An august event where Toreador luminary Victoria Ash and Banu Haqim elder Tegyrius blood bonded themselves to each other and became husband and wife as a sign of trust between the two sects.<br /> <br /> This is the canon state of the Ashirra<br /> Personally I tend to keep the DA setting mostly intact; from Cainite perspective, not many thing changed, if anything Islam just got stronger like the inflience of the middle east<br /> The Ashirra is the sect under which Islamic vampires declare their faith in Allah and believe that redemption for the Kindred was among the promises Muhammad made if they chose to follow Allah<br /> Sure while some vampires are true followers of Islam the vast majority of Kindred involved in the Ashirra follow Islam because it is convenient for them or to make it easier to operate in the Middle East. Like the Camarilla or Sabbat, the Ashirra is prone to power plays and political maneuvering.<br /> So I really never felt the need them to lose power... <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-11T08:27:22-07:00","id":1511360,"title":"","content":"Shadow,<br /> <br /> More or less what I got from WoA and the V5 stuff, also from the Veil of Night. I'm writing up the current affairs of Tehran, it's current court structure and what is going on. The material is going to be used for lore and cannon for a play by post setting.<br /> <br /> I'm just peeking about to see what others did with the Ashirra, if anyone did any sort of codified structure, and the like. When it comes to Islam and Islamic histoty, I am fairly solid on, but when it comes to politics, and culture, I have Generals to work with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12573-kueijin","name":"Kueijin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12573_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-11T14:21:10-07:00","id":1511407,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511334-ashirra?p=1511346#post1511346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Veil of Night</b>, <b>Cairo by Night</b> and in a sense<b> State of Grace</b> have the details, like history, Political Terms and status, Roads and Paths, Clans, Factions, and Bloodlines covered</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you know about any descriptions or paragraphs about the Ashirra faction in more general books like Corebooks, Storyteller's Guides, and Player's Guides ? I am looking for some basic, varied, useful, and standard facts, information, and ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-11T18:30:44-07:00","id":1511423,"title":"","content":"I always preferred the earlier idea that the Ashirra was just the term the vampires in Arabia used for themselves, while those Muslim vampires who were particularly devout and desired to protect the hold sites of Mecca and Medina was the &quot;Hajj&quot; (although technically this was a particular group of Nosferatu). &quot;Ashirra&quot; was just the local term for Kindred, and earlier WoD writers loved to sprinkle alternative, local names for their standard game terms to create exotic flavor. They weren't any kind of alternate sect. I believe they were first mentioned in 1996's A World of Darkness (2e).<br /> <br /> But in order to generate more product to create more revenue, the Revised era Developers sought to use modern day notions of multiculturalism as an easy way to pump out more sourcebooks. And therefore the (very cool) idea of a worldwide conspiracy of vampires called the Camarilla was diminished and replaced by the (very lame) idea that centuries or even thousands of years ago vampires all around the world would instead have organized themselves to anachronistic notions of late 20th century multiculturalism.<br /> <br /> I much preferred the idea that just like late 20th century Chicago could be home to a Nosferatu elder who was a former jihadist as a mortal, that a vampire who had been a Greek philosopher, former Crusader zealot, Roman senator, or Zoroastrian priest (and not currently a Muslim) could be a Prince or primogen in the Middle East. That excites my imagination in ways that current canon does not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-11T20:37:14-07:00","id":1511426,"title":"","content":"In my personal canon, I tend to view the (re)creation of Ashirra as mostly a response to colonialism. The Ashirra first came into being back in the 6th and 7th centuries as a loose organization of vampires in the Middle East. When the Camarilla was created, many of the vampires in the Middle East were initially interested in joining and quite a few did. The early Camarilla included most of the Middle East and even parts of India. The Ashirra were essentially subsumed by the Camarilla and the idea of a universal and equal vampire organization.<br /> <br /> This lasted for a number of centuries. But later in the 18th century, when Europe began engaging in conquest and colonialism in the Middle East and elsewhere, this tended to put off many local vampires who, even if they were undead, understandably weren't big fans of outsiders coming in and taking over. The Camarilla, for the most part chose to support those colonial actions. &quot;Well, it's just the mortal's doing and we shouldn't interfere,&quot; they'd say, even while many European vampires worked with colonial oppressors to make massive money off the suffering of people in the Middle East, India and elsewhere. Then, you started seeing vampires from France or Britain or elsewhere show up and try to take over or set up their own courts in Egypt, Tunisia, Algeria, India, etc, mimicking the actions of their mortal brethren.<br /> <br /> This ended up causing many vampires in the Middle East to view the Camarilla as an oppressive organization or at least as an organization that supported their oppression. Many local princes chose to leave the Camarilla, viewing the organization as one that was more interested in the wellbeing of European vampires at the expense of everyone else (which wasn't exactly wrong). They then set up their own organization, essentially recreating the Ashirra of old, which was based on decided non-European principles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T00:12:27-07:00","id":1511431,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muad'Dib</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511334-ashirra?p=1511407#post1511407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Do you know about any descriptions or paragraphs about the Ashirra faction in more general books like Corebooks, Storyteller's Guides, and Player's Guides ? I am looking for some basic, varied, useful, and standard facts, information, and ideas.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sadly - as far as I know - there are no mention of the Ashirra in core books, except the V5 camarilla","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T06:20:07-07:00","id":1511446,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511334-ashirra?p=1511431#post1511431\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sadly - as far as I know - there are no mention of the Ashirra in core books, except the V5 camarilla</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for the hint / informatiion. At least there are perhaps Dark Ages: Vampire books, art portfolios, and supplements where maybe there can be found and read some chapters, paragrahps, and also of course descriptions in regard to this Vampiric Sect / Vampiric Faction.<br /> <br /> Do you recall if it is so that Kindred from the Ashirra respect - as citizens and fellows - supernatural persons ( or otherwise said : magical beings ) other than Kindred and Ghouls ? What about Mages, Wraiths, Sorcerers, or Revenants ?<br /> Another interesting question is the possible acceptence within the Ashirra of Vampires who are not Kindred ; this is certainly a category of supernatural persons that is worthy of attention and respect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T06:37:38-07:00","id":1511447,"title":"","content":"Veil of Night in the Appendix: Against the Night (Antagonists) details this;<br /> Those who oppose the Kindred, including the Sayyadin, a group of vampire hunters, the demons of Arabia, and the other supernaturals who have a presence in these places. Included are the mages the Ahl-i-Batin and the Taftani, the mummies, the Silent Striders, the Bastet, the Ruh (Wraith), and the Djinn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T12:33:01-07:00","id":1511469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Microcuchon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511334-ashirra?p=1511337#post1511337\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The following is from a Cairo game I was ST'ing . The metaplot is quite different from the official one, by you may still find useful ideas<br /> <br /> THE ASHIRRA<br /> The Ashirra is a vampire sect that gathers all vampires who declare their faith in Allah and believe that redemption for the Kindred was one of the promises He made to the Prophet Muhammad, Peace Be Upon Him.<br /> This sect was founded in the first years of Islam and, as the religion it was based on, spread quickly from Spain and North Africa to India and beyond. Following the Islamic model of political organization, and very much unlike the Camarilla, the Ashirra has always abhorred strongly centralized institutions, and relies on a system of local Emirs or Sultans exerting direct power and assisted in spiritual matters by Ulemas and Mullahs, who form a loose confederation from all Ashirra lands. Only in counted occasions a powerful religious leader will claim the title of Supreme Imam, and his authority would only extend to those who respected him on his own merits and personal virtue. No one has claimed the title, at least seriously, since the 16th century.<br /> The Ashirra has been the majority sect in the core lands of Islam since, but it lost a lot of its influence during the era of great European expansion, when the Camarilla tenets of secular humanism and centralized power expanded all over the globe and were adopted by many younger Muslim Kindred. By the end of the 20th century Ashirra were virtually indistinguishable from Camarilla Kindred, except by the fact that barely any Kindred identifying as a Muslim could be found among the Camarilla Justicar, Archon or any other sect-wide positions.<br /> The Ashirra as a sect took back the centre stage in the Muslim Kindred society during the events of the Year of the Herald, when powerful Elders from the previous ages used their faith as a rallying point to fight against the Sabbat, Gehena cults, demon worshipers and other terrors of the night. On the mortal side, the reaction to the war on terror policy gave young Muslims a renewed sense of identity, which they brought into the night when they got Embraced. As a result, nowdays the sect is, once again, the main player in the Muslim world. The Ashirra follow the exact same Six Traditions as the Camarilla, even if sometimes interpreted or implemented in a different way. For example, the role of the Elder outlined in the Traditions is often not fulfilled by the Prince, but by the Clan Elder, as it used to be during the Middle Ages.<br /> On top of the Traditions, the Ashirra also follow the Five Pillars of Islam. Obviously, because of their condition as vampires, some of these pillars have to be adapted or compromised on by the Ashirra. Fasting is followed in a lot of different ways, and most Ashirra only pray three times a night, at the times that are suitable for then. The Pilgrimage is a difficult endeavour because of the strong Faith of Mecca and most Ashirra prepare for years before undertaking it. But no Kindred would call themself Ashirra without at least the Profession of Faith.\u200b<br /> Most Ashirra follow Tariq el-Umma (the Road of the Community), a philosophical path similar enough to Humanity to be fully accepted by the Camarilla without any problems. Its base tenets are practically the same, and the higher ones merely emphasize the beleiver's duty to the community more than to an abstract sense of self. Others, specially among the Hajj, follow Sharia el-Sama' (the Law of Heaven), a path that emphasizes the believer's duty to Allah and can sometimes justify &quot;righteous violence&quot;. More worrying are the rumours of groups of Ashirra secretly following Sharia el-Harb (the Law of War), a modern adaptation of the tenets of jyhad against the infidels; this path is considered an aberration by most mainstream Ashirra and its adepts are shunned, ostracized or actively persecuted by most sect members.<br /> Because of its decentralized structure, the Ashirra is theoretically open to every Clan and Bloodline and doesn't have a clear divide between Founding Clans and others. On the other hand, Lineage is as important as personal virtue, and having Ancestors who are well known for their devotion to the sect can help open some doors and similarly, coming from a &quot;tainted&quot; Lineage can bar many more; in this particular, Clan Tremere and Giovanni are seen as aberrations by the Ashirra Elders who remember the Middle Ages and their usurpations, and a member of these Clans would only be allowed to join the Umma if they fully renounced their Lineage and became a Caitiff. The Clanless are not openly discriminated against, by their lack of Lineage usually works against them.<br /> As much as the Ashirra would like to present themselves as a spiritual community, their are not beyond engaging in power plays, political manouvering or even open internal conflict. As their Camarilla counterparts, they try to influence or control mortal institutions and often use them in proxy conflicts against each other.<br /> There are different factions among the Ashirra, based mostly on their specific beliefs.<br /> The Progressives are mostly secular, culturally Muslim Kindred who don't follow the Pillars very strictly. Most of them are very much like Camarilla Kindred, and sometimes have either been Embraced by them or Sired one of them.<br /> The Moderates don't necessarily have the middle ground in every issue. Instead, they treat every point of debate isolatedly and try to arrive to the right conclusion, following the style of al-Azhar Mosque and University.<br /> The Conservatives look at Tradition as the source of law. They usually follow wahhabism or other conservative Muslim school and apply it's principles to Kindred unlife.<br /> The Hajj are an ultraconservative faction formed mostly by Bay't Mutasharid, who see themselves as guardians of the holy places and monsters for the infidels. Unlike regular conservatives, they have their own school of thought, that, among others extreme principles, forbids the use of Obfuscate.<br /> Banu Haqim usually follow their own branch of Islam, based in Ismailist Shi'a, which encourages mysticism, devotion and the principle of Taqiya (Simulation of Conformity).<br /> The Shepherds of Islam are a diminute but influential group of Elders dedicated to the preservation of the Traditions of Islam. They are fairly conservative but try to avoid conflict with any with other Ashirra and even monotheistic or agnostic Kindred. On the other hand, they firmly oppose paganism and other pre abrahamic religions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am Muslim and I am loving this.<br /> <br /> I will just point out an idea I mentioned in some thread here years ago, I think: a group of Muslim vampires who are vowed to protect the Prophet's - peace and blessings be upon him - descendants in the Ahl al-Bayt, and receive occasional gifts of their blood, which they consider blessed and a means to redemption and purification. All based on the hadith of the Sahabi who was told to dispose of the Prophet's - peace and blessings be upon him - blood extracted through wet cupping (hijama), but he ate it instead and was given glad tidings of protection from the Fire. Hey! That's another idea! The blood of the shurafa, given willingly and taken after purificatory service, makes them immune to fire, among other blessings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-12T18:56:35-07:00","id":1511497,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511334-ashirra?p=1511469#post1511469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I am Muslim and I am loving this.<br /> <br /> I will just point out an idea I mentioned in some thread here years ago, I think: a group of Muslim vampires who are vowed to protect the Prophet's - peace and blessings be upon him - descendants in the Ahl al-Bayt, and receive occasional gifts of their blood, which they consider blessed and a means to redemption and purification. All based on the hadith of the Sahabi who was told to dispose of the Prophet's - peace and blessings be upon him - blood extracted through wet cupping (hijama), but he ate it instead and was given glad tidings of protection from the Fire. Hey! That's another idea! The blood of the shurafa, given willingly and taken after purificatory service, makes them immune to fire, among other blessings.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for your kind words. I am no Muslim but I have had an extensive knowledge of Islam, and the Muslim community,as I worked for a few years at a school where most students and staff were Muslim, we celebrated Eid and Islamic religion was part of the curriculum. It's a religion I really appreciate and that has positively influenced my outlook of the world","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-14T02:35:27-07:00","id":1511595,"title":"","content":"I've began on writing my bit about Tehran and how the Ashirra there function, giving some details of how things were at some point. Though I didn't give a deep background, but gave some decent generals. Right now I'm detailing out the court positions and what they do.<br /> <br /> I have a tie to Khosrow I, evolving into a Lasombra lineage, and a Nosferatu lineage tied to Zahhak.<br /> <br /> The Mufti is, in some fashion, akin to the Harpy, but has more power to act. They also enforce Jizya, and have a group of Ulema who take on roles related to protected religions of the cainites of Tehran.<br /> <br /> Also have a faction forming known as the Tehran Liberation Party, though it tends to be known more as the Cainite Liberation Party. Still Ashirra, but seeking equal representation despite clan and religion, and a council who directs the court, and not a Shah.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12573,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12573-kueijin","user":"Kueijin","date":". 05-14-2023, 05:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12573-kueijin","name":"Kueijin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12573_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-14T15:51:49-07:00","id":1511664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511334-ashirra?p=1511447#post1511447\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Veil of Night in the Appendix: Against the Night (Antagonists) details this;<br /> Those who oppose the Kindred, including the Sayyadin, a group of vampire hunters, the demons of Arabia, and the other supernaturals who have a presence in these places. Included are the mages the Ahl-i-Batin and the Taftani, the mummies, the Silent Striders, the Bastet, the Ruh (Wraith), and the Djinn.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you recall if there are any details and descriptions in regard to the particular goals, reasons, ideas, and beliefs of these supernatural persons in regard to Vampires ? These supernatural beings seem to at least some extent hostile or antagonistic. However these decisions and attitudes might not be as simple as they seem initially - it is so that often there are definitely additional choices, factors, and opportunities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}